From 2acb54e5774d6ac7c182fa9da5fce808fb55f982 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 14:23:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 11/14] tests: name the chain in canned dbs, fix invoice-prefix
 assertions

Two things the python suite found on its first run, both real.

The fixture's chainparams had chain_hash updated for this chain and
bip173_prefix left as bcrt, and a dozen tests were using bip173_prefix as
a stand-in for the invoice currency. On Bitcoin those are the same string
so nothing noticed. Here they are deliberately different: addresses are
unchanged, bcrt1... as before, and invoices are lnblakert. So the fixture
gains lightning_hrp and the tests that meant the invoice prefix use it.
The ones in test_misc.py that genuinely check an address keep
bip173_prefix.

And every test that loads a canned database from an older release now
says which chain that database belongs to. Those wallets are stamped with
block 0, and this build will not adopt such a wallet on
--database-upgrade alone, by design: that is the flag cln-startos passes
unconditionally, so it cannot carry a decision about which chain a wallet
is from. The fixtures are genuinely this chain's, so they pass
--restamp-wallet-for-this-chain and say so. Twenty-seven of them across
seven files, which is a good indication of how much of the suite would
have failed for one reason.
